        "math_academy_id": "q-158436",
        "problem": "Compute $P(0\\le X\\le 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x(x+1),\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a\\le X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{5}x(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{5}({x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}+{[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}])+\\frac{1}{12}[{2}^{2}-{1}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{5}{6})+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
